<section xml:id="diffeq-notes-4-3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title> Parameter Space Analysis </title>

    <objectives>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Students will be able to...
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <ol>
            <li><p> Predict the effects of inital conditions on trajectories. </p></li>
            <li><p> Predict the effects of boundary conditions on trajectories. </p></li>
        </ol>
    </objectives>
    
    <definition xml:id="def-harmonic-motion">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A variable <m>x(t)</m> exhibits <alert>harmonic motion</alert> if it can be described with an equation of the form
                <md>
                    m\ddot{x} + \beta \dot{x} + k \dot{x} = f(t)
                </md>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If <m>f(t) \not\equiv 0 </m>, we call the motion <alert> driven </alert>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If <m>\beta = 0 </m>, we call the motion <alert> free </alert>. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If <m>\beta \lt 0</m>, we call the motion <alert> damped </alert>. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Free, undriven harmonic motion is called <alert> simple harmonic motion </alert>. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider solving the damped harmonic osciallator <md> m\ddot{x} + \beta \dot{x} + kx = 0.</md> Given this is a linear homogeneous ODE, its general solution can be obtained through the method of characteristics. To make notation simpler, let's rewrite the problem as <md> \ddot{x} + 2 \mu \dot{x} + \omega^2 x = 0. </md> Then its characteristic polynomial has the roots <md> \lambda = - \mu \pm \sqrt{\mu^2 - \omega^2}.</md> This gives us three distinct cases to consider.

                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert> Suppose <m>\mu^2 - \omega^2 \gt 0 </m> </alert>. This would indicate we have two distinct real roots and the general solution is
                            <md>
                                x(t) = e^{-\mu t} \left( c_1 e^{\sqrt{\mu^2 - \omega^2} t} + c_2 e^{-\sqrt{\mu^2 - \omega^2} t} \right)
                            </md>
                            Plotting a graph shows a smooth nonoscillatory decaying motion. Hence, we say this system is <alert> overdamped </alert>. 
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Suppose <m> \mu^2 - \omega^2 = 0 </m> </alert>. This would indicate one repeated real root; the general solution is
                            <md>
                                x(t) = e^{-\mu t} (c_1 + c_2 t)
                            </md>
                            The graph of this system shows a similar behavior, but we notice that the system passes equilibrium at most once. In this case, we say the motion is <alert> critically damped </alert>. 
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Suppose <m> \mu^2 - \omega^2 \lt 0 </m> </alert>. This would indicate one conjugate pair of roots; the general solution becomes 
                            <md>
                                x(t) = e^{-\mu t} \left( c_1 \cos(\sqrt{\mu^2 - \omega^2} t ) + c_2 \sin(\sqrt{\mu^2 - \omega^2} t) \right)
                            </md>
                            This system exhibits the oscillatory behavior we would expect, however the amplitude still decays in time: thanks to the <m>e^{-\mu t}</m> term. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>

        </statement>
    </example>

    <definition xml:id="def-parameter-space">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The set of all possible parameter values in a mathematical model is called the <alert> parameter space </alert> for the model.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Plot the equation <md> \mu^2 - \omega^2 = 0 </md> in <m>(\omega,\mu^2)</m> parameter space to visualize the damping scenarios.  
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Recall that <m>\mu = \frac{\beta}{2m} </m> and <m> \omega^2 = \frac{k}{m}. </m> Use these to build a similar parameter space diagram in <m>(\beta,k)</m> space. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>
</section>
